<SubSection title="Conservation of Mass" id="Conservation of Mass">
<p>Total mass is conserved within a system. Mass cannot be created or destroyed, and can only be transferred or converted between species.</p>

<CalloutContainer slot="cards">
<CalloutCard title="Need a review?">
<p>These concepts have also been covered in <b><a href="/cons/conservation_of_mass">Conservation of Mass</a></b> and are analogous to <b><a href="/transfer/Thermodynamic_laws">Thermodynamic Laws: Conservation of Energy</a></b>.
</p>
</CalloutCard>
</CalloutContainer>

<p> For mass conservation of a <i>single species</i>: </p>
<DisplayEquation title="" background="True" equation="Input - Output + Generation = Accumulation"/>

<p> One species of mass can be converted into another species through chemical reactions. This is demonstrated through the formulation of water. </p>
<DisplayEquation title="" background="True" equation="2H_{2} + O_{2} \\rightarrow 2H_{2}O"/>
<p>Hydrogen and oxygen gas are converted to water; however, 4 <InlineEquation equation="H"/> atoms and 2 <InlineEquation equation="O"/> atoms remain before and after the reaction.</p>

<CalloutContainer slot="cards">
    <CalloutCard title="Heads Up!">
     <p><i> Generation of mass and storage of mass are not the same thing! </i> </p>
    <p> <b> Generation</b> is the transformation of mass from one species into another (i.e., the cause) whereas <b>storage</b> of mass is manifested by a change in concentration (i.e, the effect).
      </p>
    </CalloutCard>
</CalloutContainer>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Equilibrium" id="Equilibrium">
<p> Mass equilibrium is when the total mass entering a system is equal to the total mass leaving a system. In other words, the system is in a <b>steady state</b> where mass doesn’t change with time. Equilibrium does not mean equal concentrations. It depends on polarity, pH, and molecular crowding. The transfer of mass requires a departure from equilibrium. </p>

<p> Equilibrium can occur between different phases:</p>

<Itemize>
<Item>Liquid-Gas</Item>
<Item>Gas-Solid</Item>
<Item>Solid-Liquid</Item>
</Itemize>

<p>These interphases are described below!</p>

<CalloutContainer slot="cards">
    <CalloutCard title="Heads Up!">
        <p>Mass concentration (<InlineEquation equation="\\rho_{A}"/>) and molecular concentration (<InlineEquation equation="c_{A}"/>) are related by molecular weight (<InlineEquation equation="M_{A}"/>):</p>
        <DisplayEquation equation="\\rho_{A} = c_{A} * M_{A}" title="" />
        <p>where</p>
        <Itemize>
        <Item><InlineEquation equation="\\rho_{A}=\\frac{kgA}{m^3}"/></Item>
        <Item><InlineEquation equation="c_{A}=\\frac{molA}{m^3}"/></Item>
        <Item><InlineEquation equation="M_{A}=\\frac{kgA}{molA}"/></Item>
        </Itemize>
    </CalloutCard>
</CalloutContainer>
</SubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Liquid-Gas" id="Liquid-Gas">
<p><b>Henry’s Law</b> describes the pressure dependence of the solubility of a gas in a solution: </p>
<DisplayEquation title="" background="True" equation="p_{A} = H x_{A}"/>
<p>where</p>
<Itemize>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="H"/> = Henry's Constant</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="p_{A}"/> = partial pressure of A in gas (at equilibrium)</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="x_{A}"/> = concentration of A in liquid (at equilibrium)</Item>
</Itemize>
<p>The amount of gas dissolved in a liquid is proportional to the partial pressure of that gas above the liquid. </p>
</SubSubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Gas-Solid" id="Gas-Solid">
<p>The <b>equilibrium moisture curve</b> describes the relationship between the concentration of moisture in air and the corresponding equilibrium moisture content of a solid: </p>
<DisplayEquation title="" background="True" equation="1 - RH = b_{1}e^{(-b_{2}\\omega)}"/>
<p>where</p>
<Itemize>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="RH"/> = Relative Humidity. Amount of moisture in air compared to maximum capacity (fraction).</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="b_{1},b_{2}"/> = constants describing the curve</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="\\omega"/> = equilibrium moisture content [%]</Item>
</Itemize>

<p><b>Hydroscopic:</b> Attracts and holds water molecules from the surrounding environment and shrinks when drying. <i>Examples: biomaterials, wood, soil, etc.</i></p>
<p><b>Non-Hydroscopic:</b> Does not hold onto water molecules. Water molecules behave as if the solid is not there. <i>Examples: sand, ceramics, polymer particles, etc.</i></p>
</SubSubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Solid-Liquid" id="Solid-Liquid">
<p>Equilibrium between dissolved and surface-adsorbed represented mathematically by:</p>
<DisplayEquation title="" background="True" equation="c_{A,adsorbed} = K^{*} c_{A}^{n}"/>
<p>where</p>
<Itemize>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="c_{A,adsorbed}"/> = concentration of adsorbed solute A</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="c_{A}"/> = concentration of solute A in solution</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="K^{*},n"/> = empirical constants</Item>
</Itemize>
</SubSubSection>

<SubSection title="Chemical Kinetics" id="Chemical Kinetics">
<p>Chemical kinetics is the study of rate and mechanism by which one species is chemically converted into another. </p>
</SubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Rate Laws" id="Rate Laws">
<p><b>Zeroth Order:</b> reaction rate is independent of species concentration.</p>
<DisplayEquation title="" background="True" equation="c = k^{''}t-c_{o}"/>
<p><b>First Order:</b> reaction rate is related to species concentration.</p>
<DisplayEquation title="" background="True" equation="c = c_{o}e^{(-k^{''}t)}"/>
<p>where</p>
<Itemize>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="c"/> = concentration</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="c_{o}"/> = initial concentration</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="t"/> = time</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="k^{''}"/> = reaction rate constant</Item>
</Itemize>
</SubSubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Half Life" id="Half Life">
<p>Half life is the time required for concentration to change by 50%. It is widely used in pharmacokinetics to describe reaction rate of drugs. </p>
<DisplayEquation title="" background="True" equation="c = c_{o}e^{-(\\frac{0.693}{t_{\\frac{1}{2}}})}"/>
<p>where</p>
<Itemize>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="c"/> = concentration</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="c_{o}"/> = initial concentration</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="t_{\\frac{1}{2}}"/> = half life</Item>
</Itemize>
<p>In pharmacokinetics, half life is used to…</p>
<Itemize>
<Item>Determine dosing intervals of drugs</Item>
<Item>Predict time for drug to reach steady state</Item>
<Item>Assess total drug elimination</Item>
</Itemize>
</SubSubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Arrhenius’ Law" id="Arrhenius-Law">
<p><b>Arrhenius Law:</b> reaction rates increase with temperature</p>
<DisplayEquation title="" background="True" equation="k^{''} = k^{''}_{o} e^{-(\\frac{E_{a}}{R_{g}T})}"/>
<p>where</p>
<Itemize>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="k^{''}"/> = reaction rate constant</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="k^{''}_{o}"/> = initial reaction rate constant</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="E_{a}"/> = activation energy</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="R_{g}"/> = ideal gas constant</Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="T"/> = temperature</Item>
</Itemize>
<p>An everyday example of Arrhenius’s Law is cooking eggs. A hotter pan speeds up the chemical reaction that denatures the proteins in the egg white and yolk. If you cook it at a low temperature, it takes several minutes to solidify. Increasing the pan's temperature will decrease the time it takes to cook the eggs. </p>
</SubSubSection>

